<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parmenides</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 6th–5th century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> · Also known as Parmenides of Elea</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Parmenides of Elea was a Greek philosopher of the early 5th century BCE from the colony of Elea in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>. In a poem, On Nature, he argued that what is cannot come into being, change or pass away, and that the senses deceive. He founded the Eleatic school with his pupil Zeno and deeply influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a>, who named a dialogue after him. Tradition held that he also wrote laws for Elea.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parmenides.jpg" alt="Parmenides" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sergio Spolti · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Busto_di_Parmenide_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
